  "_comment": "Golden `derive_annotation_id` vectors. `derive_annotation_id` in colca_data_contracts.payload owns the id rule: ULID-encode the first 16 bytes of SHA-256(f\"{annotation_type_id}|{source}|{time_start:.6f}|{','.join(sorted(signal_ids))}\"). The signal set is part of the identity and is hashed sorted: two rows with the same set in a different order derive the same id, a case without signals contributes the empty string after the final `|`, and two rows that differ only in their signal set derive different ids. plugins/uns/exec_edit_annotation.go implements the same rule in Go, because a human-authored annotation gets its id at the node and plugins/uns uses only the standard library. Both copies answer to this file, in tests/test_annotation_payload.py and plugins/uns/exec_edit_annotation_test.go. `source` names the author: `dataops/<producer name>` for a data processing producer, `user/<sub>` for a person, `service/<client-id>` or `service/<service-name>` for a machine identity. The `api/human:u-franz` row uses an older source spelling and stays as a regression vector; the Go executor treats source as opaque and only hashes it.",
